<section class="section"><div><span class="mono">01 / Context</span><h2 class="serif">The <em>challenge.</em></h2></div><div><p>Employee turnover was creating workforce instability in a behavioral healthcare setting. I wanted to move beyond assumptions about why employees left and investigate what made current employees want to stay.</p><p><b>Research question:</b> Which aspects of the employee experience were most meaningful to RBTs who remained with the organization, and what could be improved?</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div><span class="mono">02 / Approach</span><h2 class="serif">How I <em>investigated.</em></h2></div><div><p>I independently interviewed 20 RBTs using a consistent set of questions. I organized responses in spreadsheets, analyzed the data, and reviewed my notes to identify recurring trends across interviews.</p><p>The project combined employee-reported experiences with workforce measures used to evaluate changes over time. Because the interview guide and original data are employer-owned and unavailable, this account describes the process retrospectively rather than reproducing source materials.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div><span class="mono">03 / Findings</span><h2 class="serif">What <em>emerged.</em></h2></div><div><p><b>Protected time matters.</b> Employees identified structured, uninterrupted breaks as a meaningful part of a better work experience.</p><p><b>Recognition matters.</b> Public acknowledgment and awards emerged as valued ways to recognize employee contributions.</p><p>These themes suggested opportunities to improve day-to-day work conditions and employee acknowledgment—not simply to focus on turnover as an administrative metric.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div><span class="mono">04 / Action</span><h2 class="serif">From insight <em>to change.</em></h2></div><div><p>I independently implemented protected break schedules and a formal employee recognition and awards program. Both changes were informed by the themes identified through employee interviews.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div><span class="mono">05 / Evaluation</span><h2 class="serif">What <em>changed.</em></h2></div><div><p>Over a six-month evaluation period, I observed the following workforce measures:</p><div class="metrics"><div class="metric"><strong>40% → 25%</strong><small>Reported employee turnover; 15 percentage-point reduction</small></div><div class="metric"><strong>3 → 6 months</strong><small>Reported average employee tenure</small></div></div><p class="note">These are retrospective, self-reported project results. Without a controlled comparison, the improvements cannot be attributed exclusively to the implemented changes.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div><span class="mono">06 / Reflection</span><h2 class="serif">Research <em>judgment.</em></h2></div><div><p>Interviewing employees who stayed helped surface conditions they valued, but did not necessarily explain the experiences of employees who resigned. The project also took place in an organizational setting where other factors may have influenced retention.</p><p>The most transferable lesson was the value of listening systematically, looking for recurring patterns, and connecting research findings to practical changes that could be evaluated.</p><p class="note">Confidentiality note: This case study was reconstructed from professional recollection. Original interview notes, datasets, employee information, and employer-owned materials are not included.</p></div></section></main><footer class="footer"><span>Adrienne Ashley · Research & experience improvement</span><a href="index.html#work">Back to selected work ↑</a></footer></div></body></html>
